{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-23470",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14253746/assets/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nLet's first consider the angle ${315}^{∘}.$  From the unit circle, we see that the angle ${315}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$ Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{315}^{∘}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$ \n\nNow, let's consider the angle ${150}^{∘}.$  From the unit circle, we see that the angle ${150}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$ Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{150}^{∘}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\nFinally, we combine the results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡({315}^{∘})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡{150}^{∘} & ={(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}+(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\sqrt{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
